sec.p:
Here we briefly outline how the STA dynamics are inspired by previous models of ‘planning-as-inference’. Following Botvinick and Toussaint (2012), we formulate planning as an inference problem in a Markovian probabilistic graphical model. We assume a uniform prior over possible actions, such that p(st+1=i∣st=j):=Tij=Aij∑kAkj\begin{document}$p\left(s_{t+1}=i \mid s_{t}=j\right):=T_{i j}=\frac{A_{i j}}{\sum_{k} A_{k j}}$\end{document}. In line with Solway and Botvinick (2012) and Levine (2018), we define observations o ∈ [0, 1] as binary ‘optimality variables’ with emission probabilities
(2)
p(ot+δ=1∣st+δ)∝eβRδst+δ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(o_{t+\delta}=1 \mid s_{t+\delta}\right) \propto e^{\beta R_{\delta s} t+\delta} \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(3)
p(O∗∣{st+δ}δ=0T)∝e∑δ=0TRδst+δ, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p\left(\mathcal{O}^{*} \mid\left\{s_{t+\delta}\right\}_{\delta=0}^{T}\right) \propto e^{\sum_{\delta=0}^{T} R_{\delta s_{t+\delta}}}, \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where we define O∗:=⋂δ=0T[ot+δ=1]\begin{document}$\mathcal{O}^{*}:=\bigcap_{\delta=0}^{T}\left[o_{t+\delta}=1\right]$\end{document} as the condition that ot+δ = 1 for all δ. States with high reward are therefore more likely to have o = 1 than states with low reward, and the probability that all observations take a value of 1 is monotonic in the reward associated with the entire future trajectory. We can now condition on 𝒪 ∗ to compute a posterior distribution over future states:
(4)
p({st+δ})∣O∗)∝p(O∗∣{st+δ})p({st+δ}). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left.p\left(\left\{s_{t+\delta}\right\}\right) \mid \mathcal{O}^{*}\right) \propto p\left(\mathcal{O}^{*} \mid\left\{s_{t+\delta}\right\}\right) p\left(\left\{s_{t+\delta}\right\}\right) . \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The posterior marginals can be computed with the forward-backward algorithm, but that requires three separate neural populations for each time in the plan to represent forward messages, backward messages, and their product. Instead, we define a factorised distribution Q(st+δ) = Π qδ(st+δ) and optimise it to resemble the true posterior as closely as possible. We assume that each component of this distribution is encoded in the firing rates rδ ∈ ℝN of a single population of neurons, such that qδ(st+δ = i) := rδi.

It is common to factorise the true posterior distribution over trajectories as:
p({st+δ}∣O∗)∝p(st)p(ot=1∣st)(∏δ=1Tp(ot+δ=1∣st+δ)p(st+δ∣st+δ−1)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle p\left(\left\{s_{t+\delta}\right\} \mid \mathcal{O}^{*}\right) \propto p\left(s_{t}\right) p\left(o_{t}=1 \mid s_{t}\right)\left(\prod_{\delta=1}^{T} p\left(o_{t+\delta}=1 \mid s_{t+\delta}\right) p\left(s_{t+\delta} \mid s_{t+\delta-1}\right)\right)$$\end{document}
However, we can instead ‘split’ the transition terms across consecutive factors in a symmetrised factorisation:
p({st+δ}∣O∗)∝p(st)p(ot=1∣st)p(st+1∣st)12(∏δ=1T−1p(ot+δ=1∣st+δ)p(st+δ∣st+δ−1)12p(st+δ+1∣st+δ)12)p(ot+T=1∣st+T)p(st+T∣st+T−1)12 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered}p\left(\left\{s_{t+\delta}\right\} \mid \mathcal{O}^{*}\right) \propto p\left(s_{t}\right) p\left(o_{t}=1 \mid s_{t}\right) p\left(s_{t+1} \mid s_{t}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}\left(\prod_{\delta=1}^{T-1} p\left(o_{t+\delta}=1 \mid s_{t+\delta}\right) p\left(s_{t+\delta} \mid s_{t+\delta-1}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}} p\left(s_{t+\delta+1} \mid s_{t+\delta}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}\right) \\ p\left(o_{t+T}=1 \mid s_{t+T}\right) p\left(s_{t+T} \mid s_{t+T-1}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}\end{gathered}$$\end{document}
We can therefore define a ‘factor’ fδ(st+δ,st+δ−1,st+δ+1):=p(ot+δ=1∣st+δ)p(st+δ∣st+δ−1)12p(st+δ+1∣st+δ)12\begin{document}$f_{\delta}\left(s_{t+\delta}, s_{t+\delta-1}, s_{t+\delta+1}\right):=p\left(o_{t+\delta}=1 \mid s_{t+\delta}\right) p\left(s_{t+\delta} \mid s_{t+\delta-1}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}} p\left(s_{t+\delta+1} \mid s_{t+\delta}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}$\end{document} for each time in the plan. While the approximate qδ only depends on st+δ, fδ also depends on st+δ− 1 and st+δ+1. We want our approximate posterior to resemble the product of true factors as closely as possible:
(5)
∏δ=0Tqδ(st+δ)≈∏δ=0Tfδ(st+δ,st+δ−1,st+δ+1). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \prod_{\delta=0}^{T} q_{\delta}\left(s_{t+\delta}\right) \approx \prod_{\delta=0}^{T} f_{\delta}\left(s_{t+\delta}, s_{t+\delta-1}, s_{t+\delta+1}\right) . \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Loosely inspired by expectation propagation Minka (2013), we choose to locally minimise the discrepancy between the true and approximate posteriors. In other words, we define an objective function for component δ as the KL divergence between (i) the factor fδ(st+δ, st+δ−1, st+δ+1), while approximating the distributions over st+δ′ ≠ δ by qδ′ (st+δ′); and (ii) the distribution Q({st+δ}) implied by the firing rates {rδ}. We also introduce a normalisation constant Zδ to the first distribution. This gives rise to a loss function of the form
Lδ=KL[Zδ−1fδ∏δ′≠δqδ′‖qδ∏δ′≠δqδ′]=EZδ−1fδqδ−1qδ+1[log⁡fδ−log⁡Zδ+log⁡qδ+1+log⁡qδ−1−log⁡qδ−log⁡qδ+1−log⁡qδ−1]=EZδ−1fδqδ−1qδ+1[log⁡p(ot+δ=1∣st+δ)+12log⁡p(st+δ∣st+δ−1)+12log⁡p(st+δ+1∣st+δ)−log⁡rδ−log⁡Zδ]=Zδ−1∑ijkrδ−1,jTij12rδ+1,kTki12eβRδi(βRδi+12log⁡Tij+12log⁡Tki−log⁡rδi−log⁡Zδ). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \mathcal{L}_{\delta} & =\mathrm{KL}\left[Z_{\delta}^{-1} f_{\delta} \prod_{\delta^{\prime} \neq \delta} q_{\delta^{\prime}}| | q_{\delta} \prod_{\delta^{\prime} \neq \delta} q_{\delta^{\prime}}\right] \\ & =\mathbb{E}_{Z_{\delta}^{-1} f_{\delta} q_{\delta-1} q_{\delta+1}}\left[\log f_{\delta}-\log Z_{\delta}+\log q_{\delta+1}+\log q_{\delta-1}-\log q_{\delta}-\log q_{\delta+1}-\log q_{\delta-1}\right] \\ & =\mathbb{E}_{Z_{\delta}^{-1} f_{\delta} q_{\delta-1} q_{\delta+1}}\left[\log p\left(o_{t+\delta}=1 \mid s_{t+\delta}\right)+\frac{1}{2} \log p\left(s_{t+\delta} \mid s_{t+\delta-1}\right)+\frac{1}{2} \log p\left(s_{t+\delta+1} \mid s_{t+\delta}\right)-\log r_{\delta}-\log Z_{\delta}\right] \\ & =Z_{\delta}^{-1} \sum_{i j k} r_{\delta-1, j} T_{i j}^{\frac{1}{2}} r_{\delta+1, k} T_{k i}^{\frac{1}{2}} e^{\beta R_{\delta i}}\left(\beta R_{\delta i}+\frac{1}{2} \log T_{i j}+\frac{1}{2} \log T_{k i}-\log r_{\delta i}-\log Z_{\delta}\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
Here, Tij12\begin{document}$T_{i j}^{\frac{1}{2}}$\end{document} indicates an element-wise square root of each term in the transition matrix. We introduce a Lagrange multiplier λ for normalisation and take derivatives to minimise this objective:
(6)
∂(Lδ−λ(1−∑jrδj))∂rδi=λ−eβRδirδiZδ(∑jTij12rδ−1,j)(∑kTki12rδ+1,k). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial\left(\mathcal{L}_{\delta}-\lambda\left(1-\sum_{j} r_{\delta j}\right)\right)}{\partial r_{\delta i}}=\lambda-\frac{e^{\beta R_{\delta i}}}{r_{\delta i} Z_{\delta}}\left(\sum_{j} T_{i j}^{\frac{1}{2}} r_{\delta-1, j}\right)\left(\sum_{k} T_{k i}^{\frac{1}{2}} r_{\delta+1, k}\right) . \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This local optimisation of a factorised approximate posterior leads to the ‘mode-seeking’ dynamics seen in Figure 3B. The fixed points have the form
(7)
rδi∗∝eβRδi(∑jTij12rδ−1,j∗)(∑kTki12rδ+1,k∗). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{\delta i}^{*} \propto e^{\beta R_{\delta i}}\left(\sum_{j} T_{i j}^{\frac{1}{2}} r_{\delta-1, j}^{*}\right)\left(\sum_{k} T_{k i}^{\frac{1}{2}} r_{\delta+1, k}^{*}\right) . \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We now recall the STA fixed points:
(8)
rδi∗∝eβRδi(∑jAijrδ−1,j∗)(∑kAkirδ+1,k∗). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{\delta i}^{*} \propto e^{\beta R_{\delta i}}\left(\sum_{j} A_{i j} r_{\delta-1, j}^{*}\right)\left(\sum_{k} A_{k i} r_{\delta+1, k}^{*}\right) . \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The difference between the two is that the STA uses the adjacency matrix A in place of the element-wise square root of the transition matrix, T12\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{T}^{\frac{1}{2}}$\end{document}. The STA therefore has the form of a dynamical system with fixed points that locally approximate the posterior marginals over trajectories, conditioned on ‘optimality’ in the planning-as-inference formalism. It also has very similar dynamics to another neural implementation of planning-as-inference by Donnarumma et al. (2025). However, the local approximation with marginals may be quite poor as it neglects parts of the transition matrix. For this reason, Figure S2 and Derivation directly optimise the dynamics of a recurrent network for inference.

The fixed points of these dynamics are given by
(9)
r∗∝eβR∘(G→r∗+b→)∘(G←r∗+b←). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{r}^{*} \propto e^{\beta \boldsymbol{R}} \circ\left(\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}} \boldsymbol{r}^{*}+\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{b}}\right) \circ\left(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}} \boldsymbol{r}^{*}+\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{b}}\right) . \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We optimised these fixed points to minimise the KL divergence between (i) the true marginal posterior pδi = p(st+δ = i |𝒪∗) at each delay δ, and (ii) the approximate posterior q(st+δ=i)=rδi∗\begin{document}$q\left(s_{t+\delta}=i\right)=r_{\delta i}^{*}$\end{document} implied by the firing rates r∗. The gradient of this objective with respect to the parameters is given by
(10)
Ω:=diag(eβR). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Omega:=\operatorname{diag}\left(e^{\beta \boldsymbol{R}}\right) . \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(11)
u→:=Gr∗→+b→, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}:=\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G} \boldsymbol{r}^{*}}+\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{b}}, \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(12)
u←:=G←r∗+b←, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{u}:=\boldsymbol{G} \boldsymbol{r}^{*}+\boldsymbol{b}, \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(13)
Pt:=1Zt(I−rt∗1⊤). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{P}_{t}:=\frac{1}{Z_{t}}\left(\boldsymbol{I}-\boldsymbol{r}_{t}^{*} \mathbf{1}^{\top}\right) . \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(14)
P:=blockdiag(P1,…,PT) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \boldsymbol{P}:=\operatorname{blockdiag}\left(\boldsymbol{P}_{1}, \ldots, \boldsymbol{P}_{T}\right)$$\end{document}
(15)
J=I−PΩ[diag(u←)G→+diag(u→)G←]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{J}=\boldsymbol{I}-\boldsymbol{P} \Omega[\operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}+\operatorname{diag}(\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}] . \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(16)
∂L∂G→=[Ωdiag(u←)P⊤J−T(pr∗)](r∗)⊤. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \overrightarrow{\boldsymbol{G}}}=\left[\Omega \operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \boldsymbol{P}^{\top} \boldsymbol{J}^{-T}\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{p}}{\boldsymbol{r}^{*}}\right)\right]\left(\boldsymbol{r}^{*}\right)^{\top} . \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is given by the symmetric expression.

In the successor representation, the value function is decomposed as
(19)
Vπ(s)=Eπ[∑t=0γtrt∣s0=s] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V^{\pi}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi}\left[\sum_{t=0} \gamma^{t} r_{t} \mid s_{0}=s\right] \tag{19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(20)
=∑t=0γt∑s′pπ(st=s′∣s0=s)r(s′) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\sum_{t=0} \gamma^{t} \sum_{s^{\prime}} p_{\pi}\left(s_{t}=s^{\prime} \mid s_{0}=s\right) r\left(s^{\prime}\right) \tag{20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(21)
=rTmsπ. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\boldsymbol{r}^{T} \boldsymbol{m}_{s}^{\pi} . \tag{21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, r is a vector of the average reward associated with each state, and msπ\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{m}_{\boldsymbol{s}}^{\pi}$\end{document} is a vector of the expected discounted future occupancy of state s′ if the agent starts in state s and follows policy π:
(22)
Mss′π=∑t=0γtpπ(st=s′∣s0=s). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} M_{s s^{\prime}}^{\pi}=\sum_{t=0} \gamma^{t} p_{\pi}\left(s_{t}=s^{\prime} \mid s_{0}=s\right) . \tag{22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The full matrix M π, constructed from stacking the msπ\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{m}_{\boldsymbol{s}}^{\pi}$\end{document} corresponding to all states s, is denoted the ‘successor matrix’, and it allows us to write down a vector of expected rewards from all states as
(23)
vπ=Mπr. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{v}^{\pi}=\boldsymbol{M}^{\pi} \boldsymbol{r} . \tag{23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
M π can be learned using a TD-like algorithm as above. However, since the STA algorithm also has explicit access to the transition structure of the environment, we simply computed the exact successor matrix as the geometric series Mπ = I + γT π + γ2(T π)2 + … = (I − γT π)−1 with γ = 0.95. When computing the successor matrix, we take π to be the diffusion policy, and T π is therefore the diffusion matrix. This is similar to how the adjacency matrix serves as a global structural prior in the STA.

In Figure 5, we compare representations to an agent that computes a value function in a state space consisting of space and time. This agent uses dynamic programming to compute the value of being at location s at time t under an optimal policy for every combination of s and t:
(24)
V(T,s)=RTs \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V(T, s)=R_{T s} \tag{24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(25)
V(t,s)=Rts+maxs′∈N(s)V(t+1,s′). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V(t, s)=R_{t s}+\max _{s^{\prime} \in \mathcal{N}(s)} V\left(t+1, s^{\prime}\right) . \tag{25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
At time t, the optimal policy is greedy in the value of accessible locations at time t + 1. For the decoding analyses in Figure 5, we took the ‘neural representation’ to be the concatenation of (i) the flattened value function vf ∈ ℝTN, (ii) a one-hot representation of the current location, and (iii) a one-hot representation of the current time-within-trial. These three quantities are sufficient to compute an optimal policy.

In this section, we explain how recurrent neural networks were trained and analysed. All networks were fully connected with Nrec = 800 hidden units (except Figure S12) and ReLU nonlinearities. Each iteration of network dynamics was given by
(26)
τΔz=−z+Winx+Wrecr+brec+η, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau \Delta \boldsymbol{z}=-\boldsymbol{z}+\boldsymbol{W}_{\mathrm{in}} \boldsymbol{x}+\boldsymbol{W}_{\mathrm{rec}} \boldsymbol{r}+\boldsymbol{b}_{\mathrm{rec}}+\boldsymbol{\eta}, \tag{26} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(27)
r=[z]+ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r=[z]_{+} \tag{27} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(28)
y=Woutr+bout. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{y}=\boldsymbol{W}_{\text {out }} \boldsymbol{r}+\boldsymbol{b}_{\text {out }} . \tag{28} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, z∈RNrec\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{z} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\text {rec }}}$\end{document} are the network potentials, r∈RNrec\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{r} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\text {rec }}}$\end{document} are the firing rates, x∈RNin\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\mathrm{in}}}$\end{document} are the inputs, θ={Win∈RNrec×Nin,Wrec∈RNrec×Nrec,Wout∈RNout×Nrec,brec∈RNrecc,z0∈RNrec,bout∈Rnout}\begin{document}$\theta=\left\{\boldsymbol{W}_{\text {in }} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\text {rec }} \times N_{\text {in }}}, \boldsymbol{W}_{\text {rec }} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\text {rec }} \times N_{\text {rec }}}, \boldsymbol{W}_{\text {out }} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\text {out }} \times N_{\text {rec }}}, \boldsymbol{b}_{\text {rec }} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\text {rec } \mathrm{c}}}, \boldsymbol{z}_{0} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\text {rec }}}, \boldsymbol{b}_{\text {out }} \in \mathbb{R}^{\text {nout }}\right\}$\end{document} are the network parameters, and η∼N(0,Σ=10−6INrec)\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{\eta} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}=10^{-6} \boldsymbol{I}_{N_{\text {rec }}}\right)$\end{document} is Gaussian noise. All simulations used a time constant of τ = 5. The output policy π∝ey∈RNout\begin{document}$\pi \propto e^{\boldsymbol{y}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{\text {out }}}$\end{document} was defined in global allocentric coordinates as the desired next state (Nout = N). In Figure S11, we also analyse a network that produced a policy in ‘local’ coordinates that indicated the desired movement direction (Nout = 5). The input consisted of (i) a one-hot representation xloc ∈ ℝN of the current location in the environment; (ii) the reward xrew ∈ ℝNT as described for the tasks above; and (iii) a binary representation xw ∈ ℝ2N of the location of all walls (Jensen et al., 2024). The elements of xw indicated for each of the N locations whether there was a wall (i) to the right of, and (ii) above it. In each trial, a subset of these were present, and a subset were absent. All ‘present’ walls were assigned a value of +1 in xw, and all absent walls were assigned a value of 0. The total input dimensionality is therefore Nin = N (1 + 2 + T). Gaussian noise with a standard deviation of σ = 10−3 was added to the inputs before passing them to the RNN. A schematic illustration of all RNN inputs and outputs is provided in Figure S3.

We optimised all network parameters θ to minimise the loss function
(29)
L(θ)=λθLparams(θ)+Eτ∼πθ,opt[∑tLacct+λrLratet+λeLentt] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{L}(\theta)=\lambda_{\theta} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{params}}(\theta)+\mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi_{\theta, \mathrm{opt}}}\left[\sum_{t} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{acc}}^{t}+\lambda_{r} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rate}}^{t}+\lambda_{e} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ent}}^{t}\right] \tag{29} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(30)
Lparams(θ)=|θ|22 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{L}_{\text {params }}(\theta)=|\theta|_{2}^{2} \tag{30} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(31)
Lacct=∑a∈Aoptt−πt(a) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{acc}}^{t}=\sum_{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{opt}}^{t}}-\pi_{t}(a) \tag{31} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(32)
Lratet=|rt|22 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{L}_{\text {rate }}^{t}=\left|\boldsymbol{r}_{t}\right|_{2}^{2} \tag{32} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(33)
Lentt=∑aπt(a)log⁡πt(a). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ent}}^{t}=\sum_{a} \pi_{t}(a) \log \pi_{t}(a) . \tag{33} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Aoptt\begin{document}$\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{opt}}^{t}$\end{document} is the set of optimal actions at time t, and Eτ∼πθ,opt[⋅]\begin{document}$\mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi_{\theta, \mathrm{opt}}}[\cdot]$\end{document} indicates an expectation over trajectories induced by the policy of the agent, renormalised over optimal actions. That is, we consider an imitation learning setting where ties between equally optimal actions are broken according to the actual policy of the agent. We used λθ = 2 × 10−7, λr = 10−5, and λe = 10−4 for all analyses. The RNNs were trained using Adam (Kingma and Ba, 2015) with a learning rate of 3 × 10−4 for 200,000 batches of 200 trials (250,000 batches for the RNNs trained in changing mazes). All results are reported as mean and standard deviation across 5 separate RNNs that were trained from different random seeds and with different environment transition structures.

We then defined a predictive distribution parametrised by ϕδ={Cδ∈RN×Nrec,bδ∈RN}\begin{document}$\phi_{\delta}=\left\{\boldsymbol{C}_{\delta} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N_{\text {rec }}}, \boldsymbol{b}_{\delta} \in \mathbb{R}^{N}\right\}$\end{document} for each δ:
(34)
pϕδ(st′=si)∝exp⁡(cδirt+bδi). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p_{\phi_{\delta}}\left(s_{t^{\prime}}=s_{i}\right) \propto \exp \left(\boldsymbol{c}_{\delta i} \boldsymbol{r}_{t}+b_{\delta i}\right) . \tag{34} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Finally, we minimised an objective function that combines the accuracy across δs and the overlap between subspaces:
(35)
L(θ)=∑δ[Ert,st′(δ)∼D[−log⁡pϕδ(st′(δ))]+α1|ϕδ|1+α2|ϕδ|22]+αorthLorth, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{L}(\theta)=\sum_{\delta}\left[\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{r}_{t}, s_{t^{\prime}(\delta)} \sim \mathcal{D}}\left[-\log p_{\phi_{\delta}}\left(s_{t^{\prime}(\delta)}\right)\right]+\alpha_{1}\left|\phi_{\delta}\right|_{1}+\alpha_{2}\left|\phi_{\delta}\right|_{2}^{2}\right]+\alpha_{\text {orth }} \mathcal{L}_{\text {orth }}, \tag{35} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(36)
Lorth:=∑δk,δl∑i,j(1−δijδδkδl)cδkiTcδlj. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{L}_{\text {orth }}:=\sum_{\delta_{k}, \delta_{l}} \sum_{i, j}\left(1-\delta_{i j} \delta_{\delta_{k} \delta_{l}}\right) \boldsymbol{c}_{\delta_{k} i}^{T} \boldsymbol{c}_{\delta_{l} j} . \tag{36} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
cδki\begin{document}$c_{\delta_{k} i}$\end{document} indicates the parameter vector that predicts being at location si at a delay of (1−δijδδkδl)=0\begin{document}$\left(1-\delta_{i j} \delta_{\delta_{k} \delta_{l}}\right)=0$\end{document} if both i = j and δk = δl, and 1 otherwise. The parameters were optimised using ADAM with a learning rate of 5 × 10−3 until convergence or for a maximum of 2000 iterations. We used α1 = 10−4, α2 = 10−3, and αorth was annealed from 0 to 2 × 10−3 over 500 iterations. These hyperparameters were chosen because they resulted in a good approximation to the ‘true’ subspaces in the handcrafted STA.

In this section, we derive the equations used to optimise the model in Figure S2. Our goal is to implement planning as inference through the recurrent dynamics of a neural network. We define ω ∈ ℝNT as the vector of emission probabilities, ωt,i = p(ot = 1 st = i); p ∈ ℝNT as the vector of marginal posterior probabilities, pt,i = p(st = i o1:T); and T ∈ ℝN×N as the prior transition matrix, Tij = p(st = i st 1 = j). We use vt ∈ ℝN to refer to the block of vector v ∈ ℝNT that relates to time t. Exact planning as inference can then be implemented using the ‘forward-backward’ algorithm:
(1)
αt=ωt∘Tαt−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{\alpha}_{t}=\boldsymbol{\omega}_{t} \circ \boldsymbol{T} \boldsymbol{\alpha}_{t-1} \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(2)
βt=T⊤(ωt+1∘βt+1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{t}=\boldsymbol{T}^{\top}\left(\boldsymbol{\omega}_{t+1} \circ \boldsymbol{\beta}_{t+1}\right) \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(3)
p∝α∘β. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p \propto \alpha \circ \beta . \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We can instead construct the matrix 𝕋 ∈ ℝNT ×NT, which contains T in its lower off-diagonal blocks; the ‘boundary vectors’ b→=[1,0,…,0]&b←=[0,0,…1]\begin{document}$\underset{\rightarrow}{b}=[1,0, \ldots, 0] \& \underset{\leftarrow}{b}=[0,0, \ldots 1]$\end{document}; and the diagonal matrix Ω = diag(ω) ∈ ℝNT ×NT. This allows us to rewrite the posterior in block form:
(4)
p∝ω∘((I−TΩ)−1b→)∘((I−ΩT)−⊤b←). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{p} \propto \boldsymbol{\omega} \circ\left((\boldsymbol{I}-\mathbb{T} \Omega)^{-1} \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{b}}\right) \circ\left((\boldsymbol{I}-\Omega \mathbb{T})^{-\top} \underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{b}}\right) . \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This is the product of (i) a likelihood, (ii) a forward message, and (iii) a backward message. However, the forward and backward messages include terms in Ω. This means that the effective ‘weights’ of the network have to change from problem to problem.

We take inspiration from the form of the exact posterior, but we instead rewrite the forward and backward messages as a product of (i) a set of fixed weights that generalise across problems, and (ii) the approximate marginals qt,i ≈ p(st = i |o1:T). It is not obvious a priori what ‘messages’ should be sent between the different qt to make this approximation as good as possible. However, the fixed points should have the form
(5)
q∗=F(q∗,G→,G⟵) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} q^{*}=F\left(\boldsymbol{q}^{*}, \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}, \underset{\longleftarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}\right) \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(6)
∝ω∘(G→q∗+b→)∘(G←q∗+b←):=x. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \propto \omega \circ\left(\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}} \boldsymbol{q}^{*}+\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{b}}\right) \circ\left(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}} \boldsymbol{q}^{*}+\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{b}}\right):=\boldsymbol{x} . \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
q ∈ ℝNT is normalised separately within each time block,
(7)
qt=xtZt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} q_{t}=\frac{x_{t}}{Z_{t}}, \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(8)
Zt:=1「xt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Z_{t}:=\mathbf{1}^{「} \boldsymbol{x}_{t} . \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
For notational convenience, we define the shorthands
(9)
u→:=G→q∗+b→ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}:=\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}} \boldsymbol{q}^{*}+\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{b}} \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(10)
u←:=G←q∗+b←. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{u}:=\boldsymbol{G} \boldsymbol{q}^{*}+\boldsymbol{b} . \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Our goal is to maximise the negative KL divergence between the true and approximate marginals,
(11)
−KL[p‖q∗]=−∑t∑iptilog⁡ptiqti∗. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} -\mathrm{KL}\left[\boldsymbol{p} \| \boldsymbol{q}^{*}\right]=-\sum_{t} \sum_{i} p_{t i} \log \frac{p_{t i}}{q_{t i}^{*}} . \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Dropping terms independent of q∗, the objective is
(12)
L(q∗)=∑t∑iptilog⁡qti∗. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{L}\left(\boldsymbol{q}^{*}\right)=\sum_{t} \sum_{i} p_{t i} \log q_{t i}^{*} . \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The gradient w.r.t. G→\begin{document}$\vec{G}$\end{document} is given by the chain rule:
(13)
∂L∂G→kl=(∂L∂q∗)⊤∂q∗∂G→kl \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow k l}{G}}=\left(\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}\right)^{\mid} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow k l}{G}} \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(14)
=(∂q∗∂G→kl)⊤(pq∗). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\left(\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}{\partial G_{k l}}\right) \quad\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{p}}{\boldsymbol{q}^{*}}\right) . \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We now derive the implicit gradient of the fixed point with respect to G→\begin{document}$\vec{G}$\end{document}. Differentiating both sides of Equation 5 gives
(15)
dq∗dG→kl=dF(q∗,G→,G←)dG→kl=∂F∂q∗∂q∗∂G→kl+∂F∂G→kl. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}{d G_{\rightarrow k l}}=\frac{d F\left(\boldsymbol{q}^{*}, \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}, \underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}\right)}{d \underset{\rightarrow k l}{G}}=\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow k l}{G}}+\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow k l}{G}} . \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Rearranging,
(16)
(I−∂F∂q∗)∂q∗∂G→kl=∂F∂G→kl, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left(\boldsymbol{I}-\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}\right) \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{G}}=\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{G}}, \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(17)
∂q∗∂G→kl=J−1∂F∂G→kl, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}{\partial \operatorname{G}_{\rightarrow k l}}=\boldsymbol{J}^{-1} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow k l}{G}}, \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where J=(I−∂F∂q∗)\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{J}=\left(\boldsymbol{I}-\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}\right)$\end{document}. To compute J, we note that:
(18)
∂x∂q∗=Ω[diag(u←)G→+diag(u→)G←]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}=\Omega[\operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}+\operatorname{diag}(\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}] . \tag{18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(19)
∂F∂q∗=∂F∂x∂x∂q∗ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}=\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \boldsymbol{x}} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}} \tag{19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(20)
=PΩ[diag(u←)G→+diag(u→)G←]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\boldsymbol{P} \Omega[\operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}+\operatorname{diag}(\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}] . \tag{20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(21)
J=I−PΩ[diag(u←)G→+diag(u→)G←]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{J}=\boldsymbol{I}-\boldsymbol{P} \Omega[\operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}+\operatorname{diag}(\underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{G}}] . \tag{21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, P is the Jacobian of the normalisation:
(22)
∂Ft,i∂xt,j=δijZt−xt,iZt2=δij−Ft,iZt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial F_{t, i}}{\partial x_{t, j}}=\frac{\delta_{i j}}{Z_{t}}-\frac{x_{t, i}}{Z_{t}^{2}}=\frac{\delta_{i j}-F_{t, i}}{Z_{t}} . \tag{22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(23)
Pt:=∂Ft∂xt=1Zt(I−Ft1⊤). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{P}_{t}:=\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}_{t}}{\partial \boldsymbol{x}_{t}}=\frac{1}{Z_{t}}\left(\boldsymbol{I}-\boldsymbol{F}_{t} \mathbf{1}^{\top}\right) . \tag{23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(24)
P:=∂F∂x=blockdiag(P1,…,PT). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{P}:=\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}=\operatorname{blockdiag}\left(\boldsymbol{P}_{1}, \ldots, \boldsymbol{P}_{T}\right) . \tag{24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Since F depends on G→\begin{document}$\vec{G}$\end{document} only through x,
(25)
∂F∂G→=∂F∂x∂x∂G→kl \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{G}}=\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{F}}{\partial \boldsymbol{x}} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow k l}{G}} \tag{25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(26)
=P∂x∂G→kl. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\boldsymbol{P} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{G}} . \tag{26} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Defining ek as the kth Cartesian basis vector, we get
(27)
∂u→∂G→=eklql∗. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\underset{\rightarrow}{\dot{\boldsymbol{u}}}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{G}}=\boldsymbol{e}_{k l} q_{l}^{*} . \tag{27} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(28)
∂x∂G→=Ω[(∂u→∂G→kl)∘u←] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{G}}=\Omega\left[\left(\underset{\rightarrow k l}{\frac{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}}{\partial G}}\right) \circ \underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}\right] \tag{28} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(29)
=Ω[(ekql∗)∘u←] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\Omega\left[\left(\boldsymbol{e}_{k} q_{l}^{*}\right) \circ \underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}\right] \tag{29} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(30)
=ql∗Ωdiag(u←)ek. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =q_{l}^{*} \Omega \operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \boldsymbol{e}_{k} . \tag{30} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Consequently,
(31)
∂q∗∂G→kl=J−1P∂x∂G→ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{G}}=\boldsymbol{J}^{-1} \boldsymbol{P} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{x}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{G}} \tag{31} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(32)
=J−1Pql∗Ωdiag(u←)ek. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\boldsymbol{J}^{-1} \boldsymbol{P} q_{l}^{*} \Omega \operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \boldsymbol{e}_{k} . \tag{32} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The scalar gradient of the total loss is
(33)
∂L∂G→=(∂L∂q∗)⊤∂q∗∂∂G→ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \underset{\rightarrow}{G}}=\left(\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}\right) \quad \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}{\partial{\underset{\rightarrow}{\rightarrow}}_{k l}} \tag{33} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(34)
=ql∗(∂L∂q∗)∣J−1PΩdiag(u←)ek \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =q_{l}^{*}\left(\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}\right)^{\mid} \boldsymbol{J}^{-1} \boldsymbol{P} \Omega \operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \boldsymbol{e}_{k} \tag{34} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(35)
=ql∗[Ωdiag(u←)P⊤J−⊤(pq∗)]k, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =q_{l}^{*}\left[\Omega \operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \boldsymbol{P}^{\top} \boldsymbol{J}^{-\top}\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{p}}{\boldsymbol{q}^{*}}\right)\right]_{k}, \tag{35} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the last line follows from taking the transpose and substituting ∂L∂q∗=p/q∗\begin{document}$\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \boldsymbol{q}^{*}}=\boldsymbol{p} / \boldsymbol{q}^{*}$\end{document}. Reassembling into matrix form:
(36)
∂L∂G―=[Ωdiag(u←)P⊤J−T(pq∗)](q∗)⊤. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \underline{G}}=\left[\Omega \operatorname{diag}(\underset{\leftarrow}{\boldsymbol{u}}) \boldsymbol{P}^{\top} \boldsymbol{J}^{-T}\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{p}}{\boldsymbol{q}^{*}}\right)\right]\left(\boldsymbol{q}^{*}\right)^{\top} . \tag{36} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
To optimise the parameters, we update G→←G→+η∂L∂G\begin{document}$\underset{\rightarrow}{G} \leftarrow \underset{\rightarrow}{G}+\eta \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \boldsymbol{G}}$\end{document}, and project the result onto the set of lower-block-triangular positive matrices. The corresponding expression for G←\begin{document}$\underset{\leftarrow}{G}$\end{document} is obtained by symmetry of the forward and backward quantities.
